WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:00.330
♪♪

00:00.330 --> 00:05.368
-T-minus 10, 9, 8, 7, 6...

00:05.401 --> 00:07.737
We have main engine start.

00:07.770 --> 00:11.107
4, 3, 2...
-All right.

00:11.140 --> 00:12.976
-And liftoff!

00:13.009 --> 00:16.179
Liftoff of the 25th
space shuttle mission,

00:16.212 --> 00:18.381
and it has cleared the tower.

00:18.414 --> 00:21.084
-Whoa!
-Wow!

00:21.117 --> 00:24.387
[ Radio chatter ]

00:24.420 --> 00:26.789
-Roger, roll, Challenger.

00:26.823 --> 00:29.025
-Oh, wow.

00:29.058 --> 00:32.161
-Good roll program confirmed.

00:32.195 --> 00:34.030
Challenger now
heading downrange.

00:34.063 --> 00:36.633
-Wow!
-Oh, wow.

00:36.666 --> 00:40.069
-Ohh!

00:40.103 --> 00:44.540
-Engines beginning
throttling down now, at 94%.

00:44.574 --> 00:47.276
Velocity,
2,257 feet per second.

00:47.310 --> 00:49.012
Altitude, 4.3 nautical miles.

00:49.045 --> 00:51.681
Downrange distance,
3 nautical miles.

00:51.714 --> 00:53.750
-Challenger, go at throttle up.

00:53.783 --> 00:55.485
-Roger. Go at throttle up.

01:03.559 --> 01:05.428
[ Chatter ]

01:07.497 --> 01:09.399
-Flight controllers here
looking very carefully

01:09.432 --> 01:11.734
at the situation.

01:11.768 --> 01:13.836
Obviously a major malfunction.

01:13.870 --> 01:16.539
-A major malfunction?

01:16.572 --> 01:19.509
-We have no downlink.

01:19.542 --> 01:21.511
Reports from
the flight dynamics officer

01:21.544 --> 01:25.248
indicate that the vehicle
apparently exploded.

01:25.281 --> 01:27.583
-What?! What?!
-The vehicle has exploded!

01:27.617 --> 01:31.054
-We are looking at checking
with the recovery forces

01:31.087 --> 01:33.690
to see what can be done
at this point.

01:33.723 --> 01:35.992
-What?
-The vehicle had exploded!

01:36.025 --> 01:38.594
-Oh, please, don't say that.

01:38.628 --> 01:40.663
[ Indistinct conversations ]

01:40.697 --> 01:45.601
♪♪

01:45.635 --> 01:47.804
-Up until the
Challenger accident,

01:47.837 --> 01:52.542
NASA was a very untypical
government agency.

01:52.575 --> 01:55.745
We were inventing
as we went along,

01:55.778 --> 01:57.380
so you had a lot of freedom.

01:57.413 --> 02:03.286
-It was viewed internationally
as a fantastic place.

02:03.319 --> 02:08.324
The Apollo missions gave it
an aura of invincibility.

02:08.357 --> 02:10.660
-4 minutes and 27 seconds to go

02:10.693 --> 02:12.695
before the start
of this historic mission.

02:12.729 --> 02:14.530
-The year was 1981,

02:14.564 --> 02:17.834
and NASA was about to amaze
the world with Columbia,

02:17.867 --> 02:20.937
a new spacecraft that pushed
the limits of imagination.

02:20.970 --> 02:23.806
-They never before have sent
a spacecraft into orbit

02:23.840 --> 02:26.175
that is going to come down
as a plane.

02:26.209 --> 02:28.945
-Instead of using
a single rocket, like Apollo,

02:28.978 --> 02:31.614
the shuttle was attached
to an external fuel tank

02:31.647 --> 02:33.850
and two solid rocket boosters.

02:33.883 --> 02:35.384
Each of the boosters
was constructed

02:35.418 --> 02:36.986
of joined metal tubes,

02:37.019 --> 02:38.488
and the field joints were sealed

02:38.521 --> 02:41.023
with two rubber gaskets
called O-rings.

02:41.057 --> 02:42.792
-Look at those huge engines
getting ready

02:42.825 --> 02:46.662
to catapult this strange
assemblage off the earth.

02:46.696 --> 02:48.765
-But all this technology
wasn't cheap,

02:48.798 --> 02:51.834
and NASA had to come up
with a new funding model.

02:51.868 --> 02:54.270
So Congress and NASA
decided the shuttle program

02:54.303 --> 02:57.840
could pay its way
by carrying payloads into orbit.

02:57.874 --> 03:01.077
-The Department of Defense,
private contractors

03:01.110 --> 03:04.280
that wanted experiments
done in space

03:04.313 --> 03:08.151
would pay them
to take them up on the shuttle.

03:08.184 --> 03:10.253
-And unlike Apollo,
the shuttle system

03:10.286 --> 03:13.356
was going to be
almost entirely reusable.

03:13.389 --> 03:16.793
-They had to return
this hardware back into space

03:16.826 --> 03:19.328
as rapidly as they could.

03:19.362 --> 03:22.932
It wasn't long into the program
they realized that that process

03:22.965 --> 03:26.235
was far more difficult
than they ever anticipated.

03:26.269 --> 03:29.839
-They were under
constant pressure to launch.

03:29.872 --> 03:32.408
-T-minus 10, 9, 8...

03:32.441 --> 03:34.944
-I'm getting a few butterflies
myself right now.

03:34.977 --> 03:36.712
-We've gone for
main engine start.

03:36.746 --> 03:38.714
We have main engine start.

03:38.748 --> 03:43.753
♪♪

03:43.786 --> 03:46.756
[ Indistinct shouting ]

03:46.789 --> 03:48.758
-Go!
-Fly like an eagle.

03:48.791 --> 03:51.294
♪♪

03:51.327 --> 03:53.296
-Columbia was
a fantastic success,

03:53.329 --> 03:55.731
but NASA had to figure out
how to make these

03:55.765 --> 03:58.601
technological feats routine.

03:58.634 --> 04:00.903
-They predicted,
in the beginning,

04:00.937 --> 04:04.507
that they would be able
to launch 60 shuttles a year,

04:04.540 --> 04:09.078
that NASA would, in fact, become
self-paying or self-funded.

04:09.111 --> 04:10.513
-We had two pads up and running,

04:10.546 --> 04:12.815
so you'd have two vehicles
out on the pad,

04:12.849 --> 04:15.151
and they were going to launch,
like, three days apart.

04:15.184 --> 04:17.053
-But that really never happened.

04:17.086 --> 04:19.755
It was an experimental
technology,

04:19.789 --> 04:22.925
and they just couldn't manage
that many,

04:22.959 --> 04:25.595
so they continually fell behind.

04:25.628 --> 04:27.830
-The shuttle program never had
more than nine launches

04:27.864 --> 04:29.332
in a single year.

04:29.365 --> 04:31.968
-We have a beautiful picture
now. It's coming through.

04:32.001 --> 04:34.136
-And to help meet
their ambitious schedules,

04:34.170 --> 04:36.005
NASA worked with
private contractors

04:36.038 --> 04:38.441
to build many
of the shuttle's systems,

04:38.474 --> 04:40.810
while NASA engineers
analyzed data

04:40.843 --> 04:42.845
to see how well
everything performed.

04:42.879 --> 04:46.549
-NASA kept very good records
of anomalies.

04:46.582 --> 04:49.552
The problem is, is that
they ran into a lot of these.

04:49.585 --> 04:50.920
-One thing engineers saw

04:50.953 --> 04:53.022
was the O-rings that sealed
the booster joints

04:53.055 --> 04:55.358
weren't behaving
according to design.

04:55.391 --> 04:58.628
On several flights, especially
those at cold temperatures,

04:58.661 --> 05:01.664
rocket propellant had blown
by the primary O-ring.

05:01.697 --> 05:05.034
-The first time it happened,
they accepted it.

05:05.067 --> 05:06.335
They tested it.

05:06.369 --> 05:08.471
They thought they knew
what had happened.

05:08.504 --> 05:11.540
And then, the next launch,
everything worked,

05:11.574 --> 05:14.143
and then a few more launches,
and it happened again.

05:14.176 --> 05:16.178
-But each time,
the secondary O-ring

05:16.212 --> 05:19.181
prevented gases from escaping
the side of the booster.

05:19.215 --> 05:22.418
So rather than stalling the
program to redesign the joint,

05:22.451 --> 05:25.354
NASA waived the requirements
governing O-rings,

05:25.388 --> 05:27.156
which effectively
made it acceptable

05:27.189 --> 05:29.392
to fly with minimal erosion.

05:29.425 --> 05:32.428
-Even with the worst O-ring
erosion they'd ever had,

05:32.461 --> 05:35.698
it hadn't failed,
so they started to work on it,

05:35.731 --> 05:37.934
but they really weren't rushing.

05:37.967 --> 05:40.403
It didn't seem so terrible,

05:40.436 --> 05:45.541
but they continually expanded
the bounds of acceptable risk.

05:45.574 --> 05:48.678
-And then came
the 10th Challenger launch

05:48.711 --> 05:52.448
and a mission unlike
any NASA had attempted before.

05:52.481 --> 05:55.017
-They decided to make that
the first flight

05:55.051 --> 05:57.386
that a ordinary citizen
could fly,

05:57.420 --> 06:00.389
and that drew a tremendous
interest from the public,

06:00.423 --> 06:01.691
plus the school systems

06:01.724 --> 06:04.427
were going to show this
live on television.

06:04.460 --> 06:07.063
-At this time,
I'd like to introduce you

06:07.096 --> 06:09.231
to perhaps the person
you came to see,

06:09.265 --> 06:10.633
and that's Christa McAuliffe,

06:10.666 --> 06:13.569
our payload specialist,
teacher in space.

06:13.602 --> 06:16.739
-My job at the time of
the Challenger was the director

06:16.772 --> 06:19.041
of the space shuttle
solid-rocket motor project

06:19.075 --> 06:21.210
for my company, Morton Thiokol.

06:21.243 --> 06:23.012
-Morton Thiokol
was an engineering firm

06:23.045 --> 06:24.714
out of Brigham City, Utah,

06:24.747 --> 06:27.984
and had the NASA contract
to build the shuttle boosters.

06:28.017 --> 06:30.319
-Well, I am so excited
to be here,

06:30.353 --> 06:33.622
and I just hope everybody
tunes in on day four, now,

06:33.656 --> 06:36.092
to watch the teacher
teaching from space.

06:36.125 --> 06:38.394
-On the day before Challenger,

06:38.427 --> 06:42.331
there was an overnight low
that was record-breaking.

06:42.365 --> 06:45.101
-Got a telephone call
from one of the program managers

06:45.134 --> 06:46.702
back in Utah that worked for me.

06:46.736 --> 06:48.304
And he said, "Al," he says,

06:48.337 --> 06:50.272
"We just heard
that it might get down

06:50.306 --> 06:53.642
to as low as 18 degrees
by tomorrow morning."

06:53.676 --> 06:54.844
"Good grief," I said.

06:54.877 --> 06:57.513
"I'm really worried
about these O-ring seals

06:57.546 --> 07:01.150
being able to operate properly
at those kind of temperatures."

07:01.183 --> 07:03.052
-The mission had already
been rescheduled

07:03.085 --> 07:06.655
after routine delays,
so now Morton Thiokol and NASA

07:06.689 --> 07:08.758
scheduled an emergency
teleconference

07:08.791 --> 07:10.593
the night before the launch.

07:10.626 --> 07:14.997
-The engineers at Thiokol
were very concerned,

07:15.031 --> 07:16.832
so they began scrambling

07:16.866 --> 07:20.036
to put together an analysis
of temperature data.

07:20.069 --> 07:22.204
-Larry Mulloy
was NASA's project manager

07:22.238 --> 07:24.306
for the solid-rocket boosters.

07:24.340 --> 07:26.075
-Then we went out
to the teleconference,

07:26.108 --> 07:28.644
and Roger Boisjoly,

07:28.677 --> 07:33.349
who was kind of the O-ring czar
at Morton Thiokol,

07:33.382 --> 07:36.018
did most of the talking.

07:36.052 --> 07:38.320
The recommendation
was that we wait

07:38.354 --> 07:42.992
until it's 54 degrees
before we launch.

07:43.025 --> 07:47.596
So I said something like,
"54 degrees where?"

07:47.630 --> 07:51.967
-They had never drawn
a temperature line before,

07:52.001 --> 07:56.005
and it meant a tremendous change
to the shuttle's schedule.

07:56.038 --> 07:58.641
-It isn't what
they wanted to hear.

07:58.674 --> 08:01.077
In fact, Larry made a comment,

08:01.110 --> 08:05.114
"Thiokol, when the hell do you
want me to launch, next April?"

08:05.147 --> 08:07.483
-Thiokol's engineers in Utah
were caught off guard

08:07.516 --> 08:11.287
by NASA's strong reaction
to their recommendation,

08:11.320 --> 08:14.123
so they asked if they could have
some time off the teleconference

08:14.156 --> 08:16.192
to review the data in private.

08:16.225 --> 08:21.597
-After they went offline,
Al McDonald was visibly upset,

08:21.630 --> 08:25.367
and he said, "I wouldn't want
to be the guy that had to appear

08:25.401 --> 08:29.972
at a board of inquiry
if this thing blows."

08:30.005 --> 08:32.508
And I said,
"I understand that, Al.

08:32.541 --> 08:36.078
And you won't have to.
That'll be me."

08:36.112 --> 08:44.253
♪♪

08:44.286 --> 08:48.557
-At Thiokol, the vice president
was asking those engineers

08:48.591 --> 08:51.594
to stand up for what they said.

08:51.627 --> 08:55.064
Roger Boisjoly took the lead
in the objections.

08:55.097 --> 08:59.935
He said,
"I can't prove it to you.

08:59.969 --> 09:01.570
All I know is that

09:01.604 --> 09:06.542
it's away from goodness
in our experience base."

09:06.575 --> 09:10.546
But the engineers at Thiokol
didn't have the data,

09:10.579 --> 09:12.448
so the vice president

09:12.481 --> 09:15.518
took the decision-making
away from the engineers

09:15.551 --> 09:19.088
and asked the managers
to decide.

09:19.121 --> 09:20.523
-And they did.

09:20.556 --> 09:23.759
More than 30 minutes after
the engineers had gone offline,

09:23.792 --> 09:27.129
Thiokol managers voted to
reverse the recommendation

09:27.163 --> 09:29.932
and to launch the Challenger
as planned.

09:29.965 --> 09:31.834
The teleconference
became a focal point

09:31.867 --> 09:34.403
for the White House-appointed
Rogers Commission

09:34.436 --> 09:36.939
that investigated NASA
after the disaster.

09:36.972 --> 09:39.708
-There was not one positive
statement for launch

09:39.742 --> 09:41.210
ever made in that room.

09:41.243 --> 09:42.545
-What was driving you here?

09:42.578 --> 09:46.282
What was to be achieved
that caused you to go?

09:46.315 --> 09:49.318
♪♪

09:49.351 --> 09:53.389
-NASA pressured the folks
at Thiokol to change their mind,

09:53.422 --> 09:56.192
and it was clear to me
that we finally came back

09:56.225 --> 09:59.161
and gave them
what they wanted to hear.

09:59.195 --> 10:01.096
-You know,
we'd been rationalizing

10:01.130 --> 10:04.333
this erosion
since the second flight.

10:04.366 --> 10:07.570
-None of the information
the NASA managers were getting

10:07.603 --> 10:09.138
was new.

10:09.171 --> 10:13.542
This was not individuals
getting used to something.

10:13.576 --> 10:17.713
This was organizationally
supported.

10:17.746 --> 10:20.249
-That's where...

10:20.282 --> 10:24.253
the accident was inevitable.

10:24.286 --> 10:26.922
-Once Thiokol reversed
their initial recommendation,

10:26.956 --> 10:29.959
someone needed to sign off
on the launch rationale.

10:29.992 --> 10:33.362
-I did the smartest thing
I ever did in my lifetime.

10:33.395 --> 10:35.164
I refused to sign it.

10:35.197 --> 10:38.400
I just felt it was
too much risk to take.

10:38.434 --> 10:41.403
-So just before midnight,
McDonald's boss,

10:41.437 --> 10:44.506
Joe Kilminster,
signed off instead.

10:44.540 --> 10:46.008
After the disaster,

10:46.041 --> 10:49.211
the commission concluded
cold and joint design

10:49.245 --> 10:52.715
were major factors
in Challenger's O-ring failure.

10:52.748 --> 10:54.617
It also squarely
pointed a finger

10:54.650 --> 10:56.885
at NASA managers like Mulloy.

10:56.919 --> 10:59.188
-The commission did recognize

10:59.221 --> 11:00.990
that there was
pressure to launch,

11:01.023 --> 11:06.462
but they saw it as enacted
by amorally calculating managers

11:06.495 --> 11:10.566
who were in positions
of responsibility.

11:10.599 --> 11:12.635
I found something
completely different.

11:12.668 --> 11:15.771
-So Vaughan began
her own investigation.

11:15.804 --> 11:19.642
-No one wanted this to happen,
but intuition, you know,

11:19.675 --> 11:24.947
"I don't feel good about this,"
should have been okay,

11:24.980 --> 11:27.816
and they applied
all the usual rules

11:27.850 --> 11:31.887
in a situation where
the usual rules didn't apply.

11:31.920 --> 11:34.923
-4, 3, 2, 1.

11:34.957 --> 11:42.464
♪♪

11:42.498 --> 11:50.005
♪♪

11:50.039 --> 11:52.474
-We made a grievous error.

11:52.508 --> 11:56.812
♪♪

11:56.845 --> 11:59.815
-So the real crux
of the matter is,

11:59.848 --> 12:03.118
how do you get people
to recognize

12:03.152 --> 12:05.521
when you need
to do something different

12:05.554 --> 12:08.190
than what you've
been trained to do?

12:08.223 --> 12:10.192
-After Challenger,
the Rogers Commission

12:10.225 --> 12:12.461
prompted many changes at NASA,

12:12.494 --> 12:14.596
including an increase
in the program's budget,

12:14.630 --> 12:16.932
adding a third O-ring
to the booster joints,

12:16.965 --> 12:19.702
and moving some managers,
including Mulloy,

12:19.735 --> 12:21.937
out of the shuttle program.

12:21.970 --> 12:24.606
-But there was nothing
really about

12:24.640 --> 12:26.308
how to change the organization

12:26.342 --> 12:29.878
that came out
of the commission report.

12:29.912 --> 12:31.680
-And then in 2003,

12:31.714 --> 12:35.551
after NASA had completed
15 years of successful missions,

12:35.584 --> 12:38.921
came the 28th launch
of the Space Shuttle Columbia.

12:38.954 --> 12:42.091
-It seemed like any other
launch, but, on the second day,

12:42.124 --> 12:44.460
someone called me
on the phone and said,

12:44.493 --> 12:47.463
"You've heard about the large
piece of debris or foam

12:47.496 --> 12:49.932
that came off the tank
and hit the left wing,

12:49.965 --> 12:52.201
caused a cloud, a poof?"

12:52.234 --> 12:53.469
I said, "No, I didn't."

12:53.502 --> 12:55.304
-Columbia made it into orbit
safely,

12:55.337 --> 12:57.072
but the concern
was that if debris

12:57.106 --> 12:59.041
had caused damage
to the left wing,

12:59.074 --> 13:01.377
it could be vulnerable
on re-entry,

13:01.410 --> 13:03.645
so just days after the launch,

13:03.679 --> 13:06.615
NASA formed a special team
to assess the damage.

13:06.648 --> 13:08.984
-The decision
to ask for more data,

13:09.017 --> 13:11.620
the need for it, was unanimous.

13:11.653 --> 13:13.222
-But management
was worried about

13:13.255 --> 13:16.425
unnecessarily diverting Columbia
from its mission.

13:16.458 --> 13:18.494
Since foam damage
had been generally considered

13:18.527 --> 13:20.129
to be nonthreatening,

13:20.162 --> 13:23.665
NASA manager Linda Ham
denied three requests

13:23.699 --> 13:25.667
to get pictures
of the shuttle's underside

13:25.701 --> 13:28.570
from a nearby satellite.

13:28.604 --> 13:33.041
-And they were all put down
for different reasons.

13:33.075 --> 13:37.012
The similarity between
Challenger and Columbia

13:37.045 --> 13:43.018
was the falling back on routine
under uncertain circumstances.

13:43.051 --> 13:47.389
-In the end, NASA sent
just one communication

13:47.423 --> 13:50.058
to the astronauts
about the debris strike,

13:50.092 --> 13:52.628
and on February 1, 2003,

13:52.661 --> 13:55.097
the crew began
their return to Earth.

14:01.236 --> 14:03.772
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

14:03.806 --> 14:05.974
-Part of our
engineering culture

14:06.008 --> 14:09.278
is that you should work
to your chain of command.

14:09.311 --> 14:13.015
I will regret, always,

14:13.048 --> 14:15.617
why I didn't break the door down
by myself.

14:15.651 --> 14:17.453
-And we're ready,
Willie, no deltas.

14:17.486 --> 14:19.321
-Everything look good to you?

14:19.354 --> 14:21.190
-I don't see anything
out of the ordinary.

14:21.223 --> 14:22.658
-This is amazing.

14:22.691 --> 14:24.793
It's really getting
fairly bright out there.

14:24.827 --> 14:26.662
-They had just started
the de-orbit burn.

14:26.695 --> 14:30.232
They were coming down, and
we started seeing temperatures

14:30.265 --> 14:33.535
change higher on the left side
versus the right.

14:33.569 --> 14:35.003
-FYI, I've just lost

14:35.037 --> 14:37.172
four separate
temperature transducers

14:37.206 --> 14:38.941
on the left side
of the vehicle.

14:38.974 --> 14:42.478
-The anomalous data
confirmed my worst fear.

14:42.511 --> 14:46.782
[ Radio static ]

14:46.815 --> 14:49.384
-Columbia, Houston.
UHF comm check.

14:49.418 --> 14:52.521
Columbia, Houston,
UHF comm check.

14:52.554 --> 14:56.291
-I looked up, and I saw one of
our chief engineers in tears.

14:56.325 --> 14:58.227
"We can't get the crew,"
she said.

14:58.260 --> 15:00.262
"They've been incommunicado."

15:00.295 --> 15:02.564
♪♪

15:02.598 --> 15:05.300
"It happened. It happened."

15:05.334 --> 15:14.743
♪♪

15:14.776 --> 15:17.613
-Columbia was destroyed
on re-entry.

15:17.646 --> 15:19.748
♪♪

15:19.781 --> 15:22.484
After the disaster,
Vaughan worked closely

15:22.518 --> 15:25.487
with the Columbia Accident
Investigation Board,

15:25.521 --> 15:28.223
which concluded that NASA
had ineffective leadership

15:28.257 --> 15:29.925
and a flawed safety culture.

15:29.958 --> 15:34.229
-We are quite convinced that
these organizational matters

15:34.263 --> 15:38.100
are just as important
as the foam.

15:38.133 --> 15:40.202
-Ham soon left
the shuttle program,

15:40.235 --> 15:42.871
and NASA restructured
its management team.

15:42.905 --> 15:47.609
-This happens in many different
kinds of organizations.

15:47.643 --> 15:50.379
I don't think
that the general public

15:50.412 --> 15:53.649
got the position

15:53.682 --> 15:57.452
of either Larry Mulloy
or Linda Ham

15:57.486 --> 16:01.123
and that their behavior was,
to a great deal,

16:01.156 --> 16:05.661
determined by working in a very
rule-oriented organization.

16:05.694 --> 16:07.229
[ Bell clanging ]

16:07.262 --> 16:09.097
-Using her insights from NASA,

16:09.131 --> 16:12.267
Vaughan developed a theory that
went beyond the space agency,

16:12.301 --> 16:15.003
calling it
"normalization of deviance."

16:15.037 --> 16:17.306
She says it helps explain
how, over time,

16:17.339 --> 16:21.009
organizations come to accept
risky practices as normal.

16:21.043 --> 16:24.046
-It's widespread...

16:24.079 --> 16:28.016
with Katrina,
where the engineers were saying,

16:28.050 --> 16:30.085
"These structures
are not going to hold.

16:30.118 --> 16:32.120
We need to do
something more here,"

16:32.154 --> 16:35.891
with British Petroleum,
early warning signs ignored.

16:35.924 --> 16:39.227
-The cement used
in the Gulf oil rig was flawed.

16:39.261 --> 16:40.462
[ Indistinct shouting ]

16:40.495 --> 16:42.598
-The 2008 financial failure.

16:42.631 --> 16:44.333
-The federal commission
concluded

16:44.366 --> 16:45.767
it could have been avoided.

16:45.801 --> 16:48.337
-You had a lot of
the heavy technology

16:48.370 --> 16:50.172
derivatives and formulas,

16:50.205 --> 16:54.309
and there is a fine line
between what is devious

16:54.343 --> 16:57.112
and what's
a good business decision.

16:57.145 --> 16:58.847
-And in 2017...

16:58.880 --> 17:02.718
-Two deadly accidents
involving U.S. Navy ships.

17:02.751 --> 17:04.953
-...the U.S. Navy
cited Vaughan's theory

17:04.987 --> 17:08.256
to help explain accidents
that killed 17 sailors.

17:08.290 --> 17:10.959
-We accepted this,
what was termed,

17:10.993 --> 17:13.862
the normalization of deviance.

17:13.895 --> 17:17.299
In other words, we allowed
our standards to drop,

17:17.332 --> 17:19.301
thinking we were still okay.

17:19.334 --> 17:24.339
♪♪

17:24.373 --> 17:28.777
-We can never resolve
the problem of complexity,

17:28.810 --> 17:31.713
but you have to be sensitive
to your organization

17:31.747 --> 17:33.682
and how it works.

17:33.715 --> 17:37.252
While a lot of us work
in complex organizations,

17:37.285 --> 17:40.055
we don't really realize
the way the organizations

17:40.088 --> 17:43.725
that we inhabit
completely inhabit us.

17:43.759 --> 17:47.996
♪♪
